Q: Polling Unity3D input at 1000hz A: <p>The solution was to use the new Input System. You can set the Update type, manual or fixeddeltatime in the settings.</p>
<p>Edit: I thought settings using the new Input System made it work, but it didn't.</p>
<p>Keyboard.current[Key.Enter].wasPressedThisFrame</p>
<p>Edit: Perhaps Input.inputString would catch the characters in time, but it doesn't allow for Modifier keys (except shift).</p>
<p>Edit: I latch a handler to <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.inputsystem@1.12//api/UnityEngine.InputSystem.Keyboard.html#UnityEngine_InputSystem_Keyboard_onTextInput" rel="nofollow noreferrer">onTextInput</a> and that gives me no dropped characters at all. I'm thinking base64 encoding should be used, that's 6bit per character.</p>
<p>Edit: I'm using base64 encoding with Keyboard.current.onTextInput, and I get 20fps on desktop for 5 floats, but my mobile is stalling at 5fps and filling the buffer or something.. If anyone can help me with that, I'll accept it as answer.</p>
<p>This is my code to test in browser.</p>
<pre><code>
        function tasteDown(event) {
            event.preventDefault();
            event.stopPropagation();
            return false;
        }

        var kcnt = 0;
        var prev = Date.now();
        function tasteUp(event) {
            if (event.keyCode != 13) { 
                event.preventDefault();
                event.stopPropagation();
                return false;
            }

            var f = Date.now();
            if (f - prev &gt; 1000.0) {
                document.getElementById('speed').innerHTML = &quot;&lt;h1&gt;Samples per second: &quot; + kcnt + &quot;&lt;/h1&gt;&quot;;

                prev = f;
                kcnt = 0;
            }

            kcnt++;

            event.preventDefault();
            event.stopPropagation();
            return false;
        }
</code></pre>
<p>This is my test code for Arduino Micro:</p>
